00:08:39.690 --> 00:08:42.390
So ingesting data using
the analytics pipeline,

00:08:42.390 --> 00:08:43.758
the big picture is as follows.

00:08:43.758 --> 00:08:46.050
On the left-hand side, we
have things that emit events.

00:08:46.050 --> 00:08:48.390
They could be PCs,
consoles, mobile devices,

00:08:48.390 --> 00:08:52.470
game events from other
sources, systems, or whatnot.

00:08:52.470 --> 00:08:54.747
Those post or send
all their events

00:08:54.747 --> 00:08:55.830
to the analytics pipeline.

00:08:55.830 --> 00:08:57.413
And the goal of the
analytics pipeline

00:08:57.413 --> 00:09:00.300
is just to capture and persist
and organize all of that data

00:09:00.300 --> 00:09:02.430
in your own cloud hosting.

00:09:02.430 --> 00:09:04.740
And that then kind of
makes it ready to be

00:09:04.740 --> 00:09:08.760
used by any other service in the
future, such as Cloud Dataprep,

00:09:08.760 --> 00:09:12.110
to prepare for machine learning
models, BigQuery ML, Cloud

00:09:12.110 --> 00:09:16.500
AutoML, et cetera, or just to
analyze that data with BigQuery

00:09:16.500 --> 00:09:18.840
and feed it into reporting
systems like Tableau, Looker,

00:09:18.840 --> 00:09:21.120
et cetera.

00:09:21.120 --> 00:09:22.830
So the architecture
starts with events.

00:09:22.830 --> 00:09:26.580
They're JSON events that
are posted to a REST API.

00:09:26.580 --> 00:09:30.630
That is a Python 3
application adhering

00:09:30.630 --> 00:09:32.880
to the flash programming model.

00:09:32.880 --> 00:09:35.070
It is deployed on
Kubernetes Engine.

00:09:35.070 --> 00:09:39.870
So that means that, if
the influx of events

00:09:39.870 --> 00:09:42.450
kind of enhances,
Kubernetes can auto scale

00:09:42.450 --> 00:09:46.690
copies of that application
to however much it needs

00:09:46.690 --> 00:09:48.050
to meet those requirements.

00:09:48.050 --> 00:09:50.400
So it's horizontally scalable.

00:09:50.400 --> 00:09:51.900
When those events
arrive, it kind of

00:09:51.900 --> 00:09:54.670
augments them a little bit
between the received timestamp

00:09:54.670 --> 00:09:57.390
and then writes a new line
delimited JSON in blob storage.

00:09:57.390 --> 00:10:00.513
So once all your data is neatly
aggregated in that blob storage

00:10:00.513 --> 00:10:01.680
bucket, you're kind of done.

00:10:01.680 --> 00:10:04.060
Because you can then use
BigQuery to actually query data

00:10:04.060 --> 00:10:06.600
that's inside of that
bucket in a federated way.

00:10:06.600 --> 00:10:08.760
Federated means that
the data doesn't

00:10:08.760 --> 00:10:10.830
live inside of BigQuery,
but somewhere else.

00:10:10.830 --> 00:10:12.510
But you can still
use SQL statements

00:10:12.510 --> 00:10:14.730
on top of data that is
in new line delimited

00:10:14.730 --> 00:10:16.930
JSON in Cloud Storage.

00:10:16.930 --> 00:10:19.170
Over time, you might
notice that, OK, I actually

00:10:19.170 --> 00:10:20.490
have a lot of events coming in.

00:10:20.490 --> 00:10:23.670
Each post request becomes
a file in Cloud storage.

00:10:23.670 --> 00:10:26.920
So my queries are slowing down,
because I have so many files.

00:10:26.920 --> 00:10:28.560
So the first way you
can solve for that

00:10:28.560 --> 00:10:31.530
is by using a cloud function
that basically gets invoked.

00:10:31.530 --> 00:10:33.720
Whenever a file
hits this bucket,

00:10:33.720 --> 00:10:36.180
the function gets invoked.

00:10:36.180 --> 00:10:39.090
It takes the contents of
that file from blob storage

00:10:39.090 --> 00:10:41.180
and then writes into
native BigQuery storage.

00:10:41.180 --> 00:10:44.770
So at that point, if you
then query those tables that

00:10:44.770 --> 00:10:47.170
are in native storage,
all your queries kind of

00:10:47.170 --> 00:10:49.210
benefit from the native
performance of BigQuery.

00:10:49.210 --> 00:10:51.640
So everything will
be super again.

00:10:51.640 --> 00:10:53.830
Another way, which isn't
in the open source repo,

00:10:53.830 --> 00:10:56.760
but we've added ourselves
when load testing this,

00:10:56.760 --> 00:10:58.540
is add in a batch job.

00:10:58.540 --> 00:11:01.360
So if you use Dataflow for
this, that comes in every hour

00:11:01.360 --> 00:11:04.930
and picks up all the latest
small files in the first bucket

00:11:04.930 --> 00:11:08.330
and then aggregates them into
a second final resting place,

00:11:08.330 --> 00:11:10.848
where we then have far fewer,
but much larger, files.

00:11:10.848 --> 00:11:13.390
And if you then query those in
a federated way with BigQuery,

00:11:13.390 --> 00:11:16.920
everything will become
much bigger again.

00:11:16.920 --> 00:11:19.700
So we have successfully
scaled this infrastructure

00:11:19.700 --> 00:11:23.000
to 10,000 requests per
second at an average of one

00:11:23.000 --> 00:11:25.670
server or client request 30
seconds, which is something

00:11:25.670 --> 00:11:28.220
that you can tweak yourself
based on your capacity and peak

00:11:28.220 --> 00:11:29.180
CCU.

00:11:29.180 --> 00:11:32.848
This would support a peak
CCU of about 300,000.

00:11:32.848 --> 00:11:34.640
We also tested this
with an average payload

00:11:34.640 --> 00:11:35.900
of 10 events per request.

00:11:35.900 --> 00:11:38.340
So this would equal 8.6
billion events per day.

00:11:38.340 --> 00:11:40.920
So there's two caveats
when using this at scale.

00:11:40.920 --> 00:11:43.500
One is you probably really
want to use a similar batching

00:11:43.500 --> 00:11:44.940
setup as we have.

00:11:44.940 --> 00:11:47.520
And the second is, in order
to further optimize this,

00:11:47.520 --> 00:11:49.340
we add a caching error
at the end point,

00:11:49.340 --> 00:11:51.770
so that not each post
request becomes a file.

00:11:51.770 --> 00:11:53.840
But every 10 seconds,
it flushes out

00:11:53.840 --> 00:11:58.140
all the post requests in a
single file and Cloud storage.

00:11:58.140 --> 00:12:00.860
So what are some of the
strengths of this setup?

00:12:00.860 --> 00:12:03.223
So as I outlined, it's
designed in a modular way

00:12:03.223 --> 00:12:04.640
to handle evolving
business needs.

00:12:04.640 --> 00:12:05.750
It's super simple to start.

00:12:05.750 --> 00:12:07.125
You just start
with an end point,

00:12:07.125 --> 00:12:09.920
and then write
into Cloud Storage.

00:12:09.920 --> 00:12:12.470
Then if your demand
overtime enhances,

00:12:12.470 --> 00:12:15.020
because you run more play
tests or you have more players,

00:12:15.020 --> 00:12:16.978
you could, for instance,
use the Cloud Function

00:12:16.978 --> 00:12:19.580
to hop all of those events over
into native BigQuery storage,

00:12:19.580 --> 00:12:21.260
or you can add a
batching step like we

00:12:21.260 --> 00:12:25.700
did to concatenate these files
into fewer, but larger, ones.

00:12:25.700 --> 00:12:27.650
You have a flexible and
custom REST endpoint

00:12:27.650 --> 00:12:29.525
as an entry point, which
is set up to handle,

00:12:29.525 --> 00:12:31.340
for instance, any event,
schema, or source.

00:12:31.340 --> 00:12:34.910
So what we've done is we've
got a PlayFab event schema

00:12:34.910 --> 00:12:39.260
parser and just one for
our own event schema.

00:12:39.260 --> 00:12:41.240
And as events come in,
it just figures out

00:12:41.240 --> 00:12:43.160
which parser to use and
then write everything

00:12:43.160 --> 00:12:46.380
in the same format
in cloud storage.

00:12:46.380 --> 00:12:48.690
And we also preserve
flexibility where possible.

00:12:48.690 --> 00:12:50.940
So we use new line delimited
JSON and blob storage.

00:12:50.940 --> 00:12:52.057
That's really the key.

00:12:52.057 --> 00:12:54.140
And we've got Kubernetes
and Docker, for instance,

00:12:54.140 --> 00:12:55.610
utilized where possible.

00:12:55.610 --> 00:12:57.360
We've used a single
language, Python 3,

00:12:57.360 --> 00:12:59.360
which is very popular
within the data community.

00:12:59.360 --> 00:13:00.290
It will also help.

00:13:00.290 --> 00:13:02.540
If you've got data analysts that
are kind of engineering savvy,

00:13:02.540 --> 00:13:04.040
they can also then look
at the pipeline code

00:13:04.040 --> 00:13:05.332
and understand what's going on.

00:13:05.332 --> 00:13:06.690
So that's great.

00:13:06.690 --> 00:13:07.625
It's affordable.

00:13:07.625 --> 00:13:10.280
Because this is a deploying
your own cloud kind of delivery

00:13:10.280 --> 00:13:12.110
model, you avoid any
margin that comes

00:13:12.110 --> 00:13:14.108
with an off-the-shelf solution.

00:13:14.108 --> 00:13:15.650
So what are some of
the opportunities

00:13:15.650 --> 00:13:16.803
that we still have?

00:13:16.803 --> 00:13:18.470
We could reduce the
maintenance overhead

00:13:18.470 --> 00:13:20.178
by deploying the end
point, for instance,

00:13:20.178 --> 00:13:22.292
serverless on
Knative or Cloud Run.

00:13:22.292 --> 00:13:24.500
This way you don't have to
manage your own Kubernetes

00:13:24.500 --> 00:13:26.917
cluster and have the overhead
associated with that.

00:13:26.917 --> 00:13:29.000
You could also use the end
point to forward events

00:13:29.000 --> 00:13:30.180
to a messaging
queue, for instance.

00:13:30.180 --> 00:13:31.190
So you add a method.

00:13:31.190 --> 00:13:33.740
So instead of a v1 event,
which goes into Cloud Storage,

00:13:33.740 --> 00:13:34.790
you do v1 stream.

00:13:34.790 --> 00:13:36.638
And it goes either
to Kafka or Pub/Sub.

00:13:36.638 --> 00:13:38.180
And then you can
use that as a source

00:13:38.180 --> 00:13:39.920
for, for instance,
a Dataflow streaming

00:13:39.920 --> 00:13:42.650
job that can keep a rolling
count of, say, kills.

00:13:42.650 --> 00:13:44.360
And if that spikes
or surges, then you

00:13:44.360 --> 00:13:46.730
can trigger a blood
moon in your world.

00:13:46.730 --> 00:13:48.815
So that kind of then
enables this interplay

00:13:48.815 --> 00:13:50.690
between what the players
do in your ecosystem

00:13:50.690 --> 00:13:54.708
and how the ecosystem responds
to that on a higher level.

00:13:54.708 --> 00:13:56.750
So what are some of the
weaknesses of this setup?

00:13:56.750 --> 00:13:58.370
It's that it does require
a data engineering

00:13:58.370 --> 00:13:59.330
capacity to maintain.

00:13:59.330 --> 00:14:00.372
There is some complexity.

00:14:00.372 --> 00:14:02.870
This is not a fully
managed service.

00:14:02.870 --> 00:14:04.610
The end point will
definitely benefit

00:14:04.610 --> 00:14:06.830
if you would port it over
to a language like Go,

00:14:06.830 --> 00:14:09.380
but at the cost then, again,
of interpretability for some

00:14:09.380 --> 00:14:11.668
of the data folks out there.

00:14:11.668 --> 00:14:13.460
At high scale, you will
have a large number

00:14:13.460 --> 00:14:15.170
of small files in
cloud storage that

00:14:15.170 --> 00:14:17.610
will then disable the live
external storage functionality.

00:14:17.610 --> 00:14:20.960
So at that point, you should
either use a Cloud Function

00:14:20.960 --> 00:14:22.850
at the batching step
or at the caching layer

00:14:22.850 --> 00:14:24.740
in order to solve for that.

00:14:24.740 --> 00:14:27.050
So threats, we've got an
ever-evolving data landscape.

00:14:27.050 --> 00:14:29.420
So with new things
that come out,

00:14:29.420 --> 00:14:31.130
those might challenge
some decisions

00:14:31.130 --> 00:14:35.910
that we've made with
this infrastructure.

00:14:35.910 --> 00:14:38.600
So what are some of
the lessons learned?

00:14:38.600 --> 00:14:40.100
One of the things
that we have right

00:14:40.100 --> 00:14:41.780
now is our own kind
of event schema.

00:14:41.780 --> 00:14:43.910
So on the wire when you
send these JSON events,

00:14:43.910 --> 00:14:46.507
what is inside of each event?

00:14:46.507 --> 00:14:48.590
I'm not going to list all
of these out one by one.

00:14:48.590 --> 00:14:52.370
You can look at the slides later
and figure out what each means.

00:14:52.370 --> 00:14:54.710
But one thing that
I wanted to explain

00:14:54.710 --> 00:14:56.510
is that we've kind
of identified these

00:14:56.510 --> 00:14:58.640
as the only keys that
can be at the top level

00:14:58.640 --> 00:14:59.540
of your dictionary.

00:14:59.540 --> 00:15:02.190
Anything else would go
into event attributes.

00:15:02.190 --> 00:15:04.670
So for instance, if it's
a match start event,

00:15:04.670 --> 00:15:07.160
the match ID would sit
inside of event attributes.

00:15:07.160 --> 00:15:10.690
But by always having only these
kind of keys at the root level,

00:15:10.690 --> 00:15:12.440
you can safely unpack
these, for instance,

00:15:12.440 --> 00:15:14.750
in your first table
in a data warehouse

00:15:14.750 --> 00:15:16.250
when you ingest that somewhere.

00:15:16.250 --> 00:15:17.875
And that will
dramatically kind of like

00:15:17.875 --> 00:15:19.550
simplify the
ingestion frameworks

00:15:19.550 --> 00:15:22.230
that you will have to use if you
want to move that data, ongoing

00:15:22.230 --> 00:15:25.120
in the data warehouse.

00:15:25.120 --> 00:15:28.170
So about instrumentation,
automatically

00:15:28.170 --> 00:15:30.270
enrich incoming
events where possible.

00:15:30.270 --> 00:15:33.590
So capture things like the event
time stamp, the event index all

00:15:33.590 --> 00:15:35.820
automatically, only
requiring a developer

00:15:35.820 --> 00:15:38.960
to pass in what is unique
to that particular event.

00:15:38.960 --> 00:15:39.780
Batch your events.

00:15:39.780 --> 00:15:41.850
Don't send single events.

00:15:41.850 --> 00:15:43.470
If you can, always
batch them up.

00:15:43.470 --> 00:15:45.570
And then at what rate
you can flush those out

00:15:45.570 --> 00:15:48.702
is informed by, on the one hand,
the capacity you've provisioned

00:15:48.702 --> 00:15:50.160
for in terms of
requests per second

00:15:50.160 --> 00:15:52.077
versus the number of
peaks you seem to expect.

00:15:52.077 --> 00:15:54.870
You can kind of model
out, OK, well, then I

00:15:54.870 --> 00:15:58.450
can at most flush x amount
of time per seconds.

00:15:58.450 --> 00:16:00.150
Keep a local cache
on the client side

00:16:00.150 --> 00:16:01.650
in case of connectivity
disruptions,

00:16:01.650 --> 00:16:02.670
then write them to a file.

00:16:02.670 --> 00:16:04.712
And then whenever a
connection is re-established,

00:16:04.712 --> 00:16:05.990
you can flush everything out.

00:16:05.990 --> 00:16:08.040
Make your ingestion
end point configurable.

00:16:08.040 --> 00:16:10.290
So for instance, which host,
headers, or query strings

00:16:10.290 --> 00:16:13.080
to use for this
particular post request?

00:16:13.080 --> 00:16:15.870
And in particular, the end
point reads the query parameters

00:16:15.870 --> 00:16:16.680
of many of them.

00:16:16.680 --> 00:16:20.010
And they kind of co-determine
the ultimate file path

00:16:20.010 --> 00:16:22.075
that the file's stored
in in the Cloud Storage.

00:16:22.075 --> 00:16:24.450
So you can then basically
already organize and filter out

00:16:24.450 --> 00:16:27.420
certain event types and put
them in neat constructed folders

00:16:27.420 --> 00:16:28.600
in Cloud Storage.

00:16:28.600 --> 00:16:30.870
This can have beneficial
downstream effects.

00:16:30.870 --> 00:16:33.120
Because then I could
write, for instance, all

00:16:33.120 --> 00:16:35.520
my frames per second events
to a particular directory

00:16:35.520 --> 00:16:37.230
only containing such events.

00:16:37.230 --> 00:16:39.420
And then I can aggregate
those before I write them

00:16:39.420 --> 00:16:42.528
into BigQuery-- for instance,
in a [INAUDIBLE] table.

00:16:42.528 --> 00:16:43.820
So we're using HTTP end points.

00:16:43.820 --> 00:16:45.620
So it's always good to have
retries with exponential

00:16:45.620 --> 00:16:46.120
back off.

00:16:46.120 --> 00:16:47.900
If you don't get
a 200 code back,

00:16:47.900 --> 00:16:52.430
just wait a particular
period and then try again.

00:16:52.430 --> 00:16:54.920
Some of the tips and tricks
that we picked up on as well

00:16:54.920 --> 00:16:57.170
when actually
utilizing this data

00:16:57.170 --> 00:17:00.440
is that you can import a Google
spreadsheet as a live BigQuery

00:17:00.440 --> 00:17:01.472
table.

00:17:01.472 --> 00:17:03.680
Live-- meaning, if I make
changes to the spreadsheet,

00:17:03.680 --> 00:17:05.970
they're instantly visible
in BigQuery as well.

00:17:05.970 --> 00:17:07.470
And that can be
great, for instance,

00:17:07.470 --> 00:17:11.270
if you've got item ID to
item name mapping tables.

00:17:11.270 --> 00:17:13.390
Then vice versa, you can
also run BigQuery queries

00:17:13.390 --> 00:17:14.660
within Google Spreadsheets.

00:17:14.660 --> 00:17:17.155
And a result of that becomes
a spreadsheet as well.

00:17:17.155 --> 00:17:19.760
So this is a great way for,
for instance, business analysts

00:17:19.760 --> 00:17:22.640
to still use production
data in BigQuery,

00:17:22.640 --> 00:17:25.902
but in a spreadsheeting system
that they are familiar with.

00:17:25.902 --> 00:17:28.069
You should also check out
the BigQuery JSON function

00:17:28.069 --> 00:17:30.125
to extract data out
of event attributes.

00:17:30.125 --> 00:17:32.000
Don't use any of the
string lookup functions.

00:17:32.000 --> 00:17:32.960
Just use those functions.

00:17:32.960 --> 00:17:34.960
Because then you can just
give it a parsing path

00:17:34.960 --> 00:17:38.900
and cleanly and exactly parse
the JSON in however you want.

00:17:38.900 --> 00:17:40.820
Also, avoid
non-alphabetic characters

00:17:40.820 --> 00:17:43.730
in JSON keys, because that will
trip up the federated query

00:17:43.730 --> 00:17:47.180
mechanism that I
outlined earlier.

00:17:47.180 --> 00:17:50.230
So then finally, we
use DBT to manage

00:17:50.230 --> 00:17:51.940
and source control
all of our SQL code

00:17:51.940 --> 00:17:55.390
and to document
everything as well.

00:17:55.390 --> 00:17:59.440
DBT kind of out of the box
makes every query a model.

00:17:59.440 --> 00:18:01.580
And then you reference
other models,

00:18:01.580 --> 00:18:03.760
which means the entire
lineage graph or lineage

00:18:03.760 --> 00:18:05.890
system is preserved with
DBT invisible, which

00:18:05.890 --> 00:18:08.680
is very nice for maintaining
all of these queries.

00:18:08.680 --> 00:18:10.570
And also, if you use
the schema.yaml file,

00:18:10.570 --> 00:18:13.725
you can generate docs
just out of the box.

00:18:13.725 --> 00:18:15.850
And that will really allow
new [INAUDIBLE] analysts

00:18:15.850 --> 00:18:17.680
that are just
coming in, and they

00:18:17.680 --> 00:18:19.690
need to get to know
your schema really well.

00:18:19.690 --> 00:18:21.815
It will really help them
to have that as a resource

00:18:21.815 --> 00:18:24.490
to figure out how to run the
queries, what query [INAUDIBLE]

00:18:24.490 --> 00:18:26.543
tables to look at, et cetera.

00:18:26.543 --> 00:18:28.460
Then finally, you should
also definitely adopt

00:18:28.460 --> 00:18:30.440
the GDPR strategy.

00:18:30.440 --> 00:18:33.010
So two examples-- but
it's really up to you,

00:18:33.010 --> 00:18:35.200
ultimately, how you
want to approach this.

00:18:35.200 --> 00:18:37.383
But one could be you add
a method to the end point,

00:18:37.383 --> 00:18:38.800
and then you route
all your events

00:18:38.800 --> 00:18:41.290
that contain personal
identifiable information

00:18:41.290 --> 00:18:42.800
only to your data warehouse.

00:18:42.800 --> 00:18:44.530
So you avoid blob storage.

00:18:44.530 --> 00:18:47.310
Because at that point, all of
that data that is sensitive

00:18:47.310 --> 00:18:49.790
is in a SQL kind
of like ecosystem.

00:18:49.790 --> 00:18:52.090
So you can run select
star from exports

00:18:52.090 --> 00:18:56.410
and delete from for any
forget me kind of requests.

00:18:56.410 --> 00:18:58.570
Alternatively, if you
use the batching setup,

00:18:58.570 --> 00:19:01.260
what you could do is you
set a retention policy

00:19:01.260 --> 00:19:03.120
on the first bucket for
four to eight weeks.

00:19:03.120 --> 00:19:04.870
And then when the
batching stuff comes in,

00:19:04.870 --> 00:19:07.480
for instance with Dataflow, you
have the data in memory again.

00:19:07.480 --> 00:19:10.180
And that's where you can then do
any scrubbing or anonymization

00:19:10.180 --> 00:19:11.950
of that data before
you then move it over

00:19:11.950 --> 00:19:13.760
into its final resting place.

00:19:13.760 --> 00:19:17.150
But then you can kind of
cope with it like that.

00:19:17.150 --> 00:19:18.590
So that is everything.

00:19:18.590 --> 00:19:19.850
Thanks for watching the video.

00:19:19.850 --> 00:19:21.410
I hope you find it informative.

00:19:21.410 --> 00:19:23.890
And if you have any questions
about it or suggestions,

00:19:23.890 --> 00:19:27.640
then, yeah, feel free
to go to our website,

00:19:27.640 --> 00:19:29.680
find out the forms,
leave a question there.

00:19:29.680 --> 00:19:32.410
Or find me on LinkedIn and
just ping me a message,

00:19:32.410 --> 00:19:33.970
and I will get back to you.

00:19:33.970 --> 00:19:37.020
